    angle: `East Cesar Chavez has been Austin\'s most-changed residential submarket over the past decade. New-build density has infilled aggressively into 1920s bungalow blocks, creating a distinctive block-by-block character where a 2024 modern sits next to a preserved 1928 shotgun house. That new-vs-old bifurcation is the single most important thing to understand about this neighborhood.\n\nThe comp implication: a "median" for East Cesar Chavez is meaningless. A restored 1200 sqft bungalow trades in a completely different band than a new-build 1800 sqft SFR on the same block. If your Realtor is showing you $/sqft comps across product types, they\'re doing you a disservice — you need bungalow comps for bungalows and new-build comps for new-builds.`,
    streetLevel: `East Cesar Chavez has real gentrification-adjacent transaction dynamics that buyers and sellers both need to understand. Long-time family sellers (many in the neighborhood since the 1970s-1980s) often price to what they know from decades ago rather than current market. That can be a value opportunity for the right buyer, but it also creates emotional negotiation dynamics — these are family homes with generational significance, not investment properties.\n\nOn the buy side, East Cesar Chavez blocks are still transitioning. Some blocks are 80% new-build (already through the transition); others are 30% new-build (still transitioning, higher upside but also higher block-level uncertainty). Understand exactly where a specific block sits before you commit — it changes both current price and the 5-year appreciation curve.`,
